WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.337
NEIL DEGRASSE TYSON:
We all feel the weight

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:07.207
of the shadows on our future.

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:11.411
But in another time, every
bit as ominous as our own,

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:15.081
there were those who could
see a way through the darkness

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:19.386
to find a star to steer by.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:25.458
Carl Sagan wrote, "I was
a child in a time of hope.

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:28.828
I wanted to be a scientist
from my earliest school days.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:32.432
The crystallizing moment
came when I first caught on

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:36.903
that the stars are mighty suns,
when it first dawned on me how

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.708
staggeringly far away they must
be to appear as mere points

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.543
of light in the sky.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.613
I'm not sure I even knew the
meaning of the word 'science'

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:49.883
then, but I wanted
somehow to immerse

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.619
myself in all that grandeur.

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:56.890
I was gripped by the splendor
of the universe, transfixed

00:00:56.956 --> 00:01:00.693
by the prospect of understanding
how things really work,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.630
of helping to uncover
deep mysteries,

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:09.636
of exploring new worlds,
maybe even literally.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:18.545
It has been my good fortune
to have had that dream in part

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:19.345
fulfilled.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:29.255
For me, the romance
of science remains

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:33.993
as appealing and new
as it was on that day

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:38.865
when I was shown the
wonders of the 1939

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:43.169
New York World's Fair."

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:47.173
[fireworks bursting]

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:54.047
This is where the
future became a place.

00:01:54.113 --> 00:01:57.750
But how could there
be hope in 1939?

00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:01.721
The angriest voices had taken
the world stage, preaching

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.224
hatred and tribal division.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.826
The most cataclysmic
war in history,

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:10.930
which would take the lives
of 16 million human beings,

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:13.433
was only just beginning.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.336
Yet even as darkness
descended, it

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:18.605
was possible to
awaken the young Carl

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.941
Sagan and his contemporaries
with a thrilling vision

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:26.880
of the future one that was
powerful enough to inspire many

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:30.884
of them to do the years of
hard work required to become

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.353
scientists and engineers.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.749
The miracle of television
became a reality to the public

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:05.485
at the 1939 World's Fair.

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:08.221
We had learned to
manipulate electrons

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:12.358
into what would become a
civilization-altering force.

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:16.462
This working model of a TV set
was transparent to convince

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:18.898
the skeptics that
what they were seeing

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:21.267
was not just
motion-picture images.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:23.202
The images on the
television screen

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:29.275
were actually live signals
from across time and space.

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:32.712
A possible world
of high technology

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:34.280
was first glimpsed here.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:38.184
[emotive string music]

